{"id": "f5ac418b-f4a4-4170-a923-e8658ae1a308", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T23:18:55.857843", "source": "user", "parent_id": "6722c3c7-d0b0-4921-941d-9170357db59c", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Inspection and sketch look sound to me. Your reported findings are consistent with a design-only brief — no repos or compute required, so leaning on prior knowledge for library details is acceptable as long as it stays flagged as unverified, which you did. Decisions on your open points: (1) Metric accounting: agreed, episode-steps/s as the headline (one parallel step across all agents counts as one step), agent-steps/s as secondary; for the vectorized JaxMARL arm, report both per-call wall-clock throughput and labeled num_envs x calls/s, so the comparison is transparent either way. (2) Primary comparison is CPU vs CPU with JAX_PLATFORMS=cpu; the GPU arm stays optional and conditional, described as such in the design. (3) Environment parity: keep the shape + seeded reward/termination trace parity check as a gating control — if the traces diverge, the benchmark is invalid and the design should say what to check first (obs construction, reward normalization, termination semantics). (4) Reset semantics: since auto-reset behavior may differ, state explicitly in the design that reset cost is amortized inside the steady-state loop for JaxMARL and handled per-episode (done -> reset) for PettingZoo, and note this as a possible source of asymmetry. (5) Version/naming uncertainty (simple_spread_v2 vs v3): fine to keep as an assumption; the design should specify how a version would be pinned if execution ever happened. Now produce the final deliverable: the complete experiment design with full rationale, controls, step-by-step measurement procedure, analysis plan (statistics over repeated runs, how speedup will be reported, how vectorized batch size is swept), limitations/validity threats, and a feasibility statement under the inspected runtime. Design only — do not claim any experiment was run.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "f3d6a194-6961-44b9-a857-8d27a5847004", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T23:19:44.991429", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "f5ac418b-f4a4-4170-a923-e8658ae1a308", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Understood — all five decisions incorporated. Here is the complete experiment design."}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher has approved. Now produce the full experiment design as stage=result. Design only, comprehensive. Include: rationale, controls, step-by-step measurement procedure, analysis plan (statistics, speedup reporting, batch sweep), limitations/validity threats, feasibility statement under inspected runtime, version pinning, reset semantics asymmetry note, parity gating check with troubleshooting.\n\nLet me write a thorough but organized design.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design: JaxMARL MPE Simple Spread vs. PettingZoo MPE (CPU) — Steps-per-Second Under Random Actions\n\n**Status: DESIGN ONLY. No experiment was executed and no results are claimed.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research question and hypotheses\n\n**RQ:** How does JaxMARL's JAX-native implementation of MPE Simple Spread compare to the original CPU-based MPE (PettingZoo) in steps-per-second under uniformly random actions?\n\n**H1 (primary):** With JIT-compiled, vectorized stepping on CPU, JaxMARL achieves higher episode-steps/s than PettingZoo's CPU implementation at matched environment configuration.\n\n**H2 (secondary, batch sweep):** JaxMARL throughput increases with `num_envs` (vectorized batch size) toward a saturation point; the design identifies where diminishing returns begin on the target CPU.\n\n**H3 (descriptive, optional GPU arm):** On a GPU host, JaxMARL throughput exceeds the CPU numbers by a reported factor (described conditionally; not a gating claim).\n\n## 2. Environment configuration (parity contract)\n\nBoth implementations configured identically; any deviation voids the run:\n\n| Parameter | Value | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Agents (N) | 3 | MPE simple_spread default |\n| Landmarks | 3 | Default (one per agent) |\n| Episode length | `max_cycles=25` | Same for both arms |\n| Action space | Discrete(5) | Verified by printing both sides' action spaces; PettingZoo uses MultiDiscrete([5]*3) per-agent in parallel API — the harness maps a single 5-way action per agent per cycle on both sides |\n| Observation space | Shape/ dtype compared at reset; both are local (pos, vel, landmark deltas, comms) continuous vectors | Shapes must match modulo known JaxMARL flattening; assert equal feature count |\n| Random policy | Single seeded uniform sampler over {0..4}, one action per agent per cycle, identical seed streams on both sides (numpy `default_rng(seed)` on CPU; `jax.random` with equivalent seed for JaxMARL's batched action tensor) | |\n| Seeds | 5 independent episode seeds; seed 0 used for parity check | |\n\n**Gating parity check (pre-benchmark, per §5 step 3):** under seed 0 and the same seeded action sequence, step both environments for one full episode (25 cycles) and compare per-cycle (a) observation shapes, (b) per-agent reward vectors, (c) termination/done flags, within float tolerance (rtol=1e-4). **If traces diverge, the benchmark is invalid.** Debug order: (1) observation construction ordering (agent/landmark indexing, which quantities are included and their concatenation order); (2) reward normalization (`local_ratio` handling — PettingZoo blends local/global rewards; confirm JaxMARL's weighting matches); (3) termination/episode-boundary semantics (collisions, boundary conditions, `done` vs `termination`/`truncation` split in JAX envs); (4) action-space mapping (discrete one-hot vs index). Parity is evaluated on rewards and dones, not exact observations, because internal representation ordering may legitimately differ while dynamics match; if observations differ but reward/done traces match within tolerance, the benchmark proceeds with a documented note.\n\n## 3. Metric definitions (fixed by researcher decision)\n\n- **Episode-step:** one step of the environment, i.e., one parallel step across all N agents. This is the headline unit.\n- **Agent-step:** one single-agent step (N agent-steps = 1 episode-step in MPE). Reported secondarily.\n- **JaxMARL vectorized arm:** per call, `num_envs` episode-steps occur. Report:\n  - **labeled batched throughput:** `num_envs × calls/s` episode-steps/s, always annotated with the `num_envs` used;\n  - **per-call wall-clock:** seconds per `step()` call and per-call latency distribution, so a single-env-equivalent figure can be derived transparently.\n- **Reset accounting (explicit asymmetry note, per researcher decision 4):**\n  - *JaxMARL:* auto-reset is amortized inside the steady-state loop (batched envs reset inline when done, as is idiomatic for JAX vectorized envs); reset cost therefore appears inside steady-state numbers.\n  - *PettingZoo:* reset is handled per-episode (`done → env.reset()`); with `max_cycles=25`, a full episode includes 1 reset + 25 steps, and steady-state measurement runs many back-to-back episodes so the per-episode reset cost is also inside the steady-state number.\n  - Because the amortization mechanics differ (batched inline vs per-episode loop), reset contributes asymmetrically; a supplementary measurement reports **reset-only cost** for each side (time `reset()` alone, ≥50 repetitions) so the asymmetry can be quantified and, if material, a reset-excluded sensitivity figure is also reported.\n\n## 4. Hardware pinning\n\n- **Primary platform:** CPU-only. `JAX_PLATFORMS=cpu` (and `--xla_force_host_platform_device_count` left default) for the JaxMARL arm; PettingZoo runs on plain numpy threads.\n- **Affinity:** both arms pinned to the same fixed CPU set, e.g., `taskset -c 0-15` (16 cores), with intra-process thread counts controlled: `XLA_FLAGS=--xla_cpu_multi_thread_eigen=true` and thread pool sized to the pinned set; numpy/OpenBLAS threads (`OMP_NUM_THREADS`) matched to the same count. A thread-count sensitivity spot-check (1, 4, 16 threads) is included as a secondary factor since vectorized JAX-on-CPU benefits from intra-op parallelism while numpy MPE is largely single-threaded per env.\n- **GPU arm (optional, conditional):** executed only on a host with a working CUDA driver; same protocol with `JAX_PLATFORMS=gpu`, batch sizes scaled up (e.g., 1k–65k envs), CPU-only results reported alongside. If no GPU is available (as in the inspected runtime), the design stands complete without it.\n- Environment hygiene: fixed clock source where available, background load checked (idle machine), and `block_until_ready()` (or `jax.block_until_ready(result)`) before stopping the JAX-side clock to exclude async-dispatch distortion.\n\n## 5. Measurement procedure (step-by-step)\n\n**Per arm, per run:**\n1. Set seed, pin affinity, instantiate environment(s) with the parity-contract config.\n2. **Warm-up (untimed):** perform W steps (JaxMARL: enough calls to trigger JIT compilation of `reset` and `step`, plus 20 steady steps after compile; PettingZoo: 20 steps) — discard all timing.\n3. **Steady-state measurement:** run until either K episode-steps or a wall-clock budget T is reached (whichever first; defaults K = 2,000 episode-steps per PettingZoo run, T = 10 s per run — both arms use the same rule). During the loop:\n   - sample uniform random actions from the seeded policy each cycle;\n   - JaxMARL: batched `step(actions)`; count `num_envs` episode-steps per call; auto-reset amortized;\n   - PettingZoo: `step()` per parallel env, then per-agent reward/`done` consumption; on done → `reset(seed=s')`;\n   - record per-step timestamps (coarse loop-level timing is primary; per-step deltas recorded for distributional checks).\n4. Record: total wall-clock (after `block_until_ready` on the JAX side), episode-steps completed, agent-steps, number of resets, JIT compile time (JaxMARL, from warm-up instrumentation), and `num_envs`.\n5. **Repeats:** 5 independent runs per configuration point (fresh process per run where feasible, to isolate JIT/allocator state; at minimum fresh env instances), each with a distinct seed.\n\n**Batch sweep (JaxMARL only):** `num_envs ∈ {1, 8, 64, 256, 1024, 4096, 16384}` (log-spaced; adjusted if memory-bound on the host). PettingZoo's equivalent point is `num_envs = 1` (its native mode). This yields the H2 saturation curve and an honest apples-to-apples point at `num_envs=1` in addition to the labeled batched figures.\n\n**Reset-only measurement:** 50 timed `reset()` calls per arm (JaxMARL: batched at each swept `num_envs`), reported separately.\n\n## 6. Analysis plan\n\n- **Per configuration point:** mean and standard deviation of episode-steps/s across the 5 runs; also report median and min/max (throughput timings are right-skewed; the median is the robust central estimate).\n- **Headline comparison:** JaxMARL best steady-state configuration (argmax over the batch sweep, stated explicitly with its `num_envs`) vs. PettingZoo `num_envs=1`, at `num_envs=1` vs `num_envs=1` for the implementation-only comparison. **Speedup** = median(JaxMARL steps/s) / median(PettingZoo steps/s), reported with a bootstrap 95% CI (10,000 resamples over runs).\n- **Significance:** Welch's t-test on log-transformed per-run throughputs (α = 0.05), given n=5 per arm; also report the effect size as the ratio of medians with CI rather than relying on the p-value.\n- **Batch sweep:** plot episode-steps/s and per-call latency vs `num_envs` (log-x); report saturation `num_envs` (smallest batch within 5% of peak) and throughput-per-unit-work trends.\n- **Sensitivity figures:** reset-excluded throughput; thread-count sweep; GPU arm if available.\n- All raw per-step timestamps and per-run summaries are emitted as CSV/JSON with config metadata (versions, seeds, thread counts, affinity) so the analysis is reproducible.\n\n## 7. Version pinning (conditional on execution)\n\nIf executed, pin and record: `jaxmarl==<X>`, `jax==<Y>`, `pettingzoo==<Z>` (with the MPE variant actually imported — `simple_spread_v3` preferred; if only `v2` is available, run it and record the substitution as a validity note), `numpy`, Python version, and commit hashes if installed from source. A `requirements.txt` freeze accompanies results. Since library details here rest on prior knowledge (unverified offline), the design treats exact version behavior as a check-at-execution item, not an assumption.\n\n## 8. Controls and validity threats\n\n**Controls:** parity gating check (§2); identical seeds and random policy; identical config; pinned CPU set and matched thread counts; warm-up excluded; same K/T steady-state rule; reset-only supplementary measurement.\n\n**Threats / limitations (explicit):**\n1. **Vectorization accounting:** batched JaxMARL steps are not equivalent work to a single PettingZoo env; mitigated by labeled reporting and the `num_envs=1` point.\n2. **Reset amortization asymmetry** (per §3); mitigated by reset-only figures and a reset-excluded sensitivity view.\n3. **JIT compile amortization:** compile cost is excluded from steady state but is a real one-time cost; reported separately. Single-env JIT overhead (retracing, dispatch) may dominate at `num_envs=1` — this is itself a finding, not a bug.\n4. **Random-action policies don't exercise all state space** (e.g., collision-heavy trajectories): acceptable for a throughput question, but noted as not a policy-learning benchmark.\n5. **Version drift:** MPE v2 vs v3 differences; JAX version performance changes. Mitigated by pinning.\n6. **Hardware specificity:** results are machine-specific (this design targets the inspected 224-vCPU Xeon Platinum 8570 host, CPU-only); not portable claims.\n7. **Timing granularity:** per-step `perf_counter` overhead is negligible at the loop level but per-step distributions should be interpreted cautiously; loop-level totals are primary.\n8. **Prior-knowledge library details** (APIs, defaults) are unverified in the offline inspected runtime and must be validated at execution (§7).\n\n## 9. Feasibility under the inspected runtime\n\nThe inspected runtime (Python 3.14.4, 224 vCPUs, ~2 TB RAM, **no GPU**, **no network**, jaxmarl/pettingzoo **not installed**) **cannot execute this benchmark as-is**: dependencies are uninstallable offline. Feasibility therefore depends on an execution environment with (a) network or pre-installed `jax`, `jaxmarl`, `pettingzoo`, `numpy`; (b) pinned CPU access. The 224-vCPU host comfortably supports the full batch sweep (16k envs of a lightweight MPE env fits easily in RAM); the GPU arm requires a different host and remains optional. Under the design-only brief this is acceptable — the design is complete and executable wherever the dependencies exist.\n\n**Deliverables of a future execution:** per-run CSVs, summary statistics table (median episode-steps/s ± std, speedup with CI, per-call latency), batch-sweep curve, reset-only timings, parity-check log, and environment freeze. No experiment has been run.", "evidence": "Repository observations: /workspace contains only task.json confirming the design-only brief (\"track.md: Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"); no jaxmarl or pettingzoo installed (ModuleNotFoundError for both; pip list empty of relevant packages); network disabled (pip download fails with connection error); Python 3.14.4. Hardware inspected: 224 vCPU Intel Xeon Platinum 8570, ~2011 GB RAM (1893 GB available), nvidia-smi fails (no GPU), gpu_enabled=false. Design incorporates all five researcher decisions: (1) dual metric accounting with labeled batched throughput and per-call wall-clock; (2) CPU-vs-CPU primary with conditional GPU arm; (3) shape + seeded reward/termination trace parity check as a gating control with a defined debug order (obs construction, reward normalization/local_ratio, termination semantics, action mapping); (4) explicit statement of amortized-inline reset (JaxMARL) vs per-episode reset (PettingZoo) plus reset-only supplementary timing; (5) version-pinning procedure (requirements freeze, record imported variant v2 vs v3, commit hashes if from source). Measurement protocol: 5 runs per point, warm-up excluded, block_until_ready before stopping JAX clock, steady-state rule K=2000 episode-steps or T=10s, batch sweep num_envs in {1,8,64,256,1024,4096,16384}, thread/affinity pinning with sensitivity spot-check. Analysis: median throughput with bootstrap 95% CI, Welch's t-test on log-transformed run throughputs, saturation-point identification, reset-excluded and thread-count sensitivity figures. Feasibility: inspected runtime cannot execute (no deps, no network); design executable on any host with jax/jaxmarl/pettingzoo installed; 224-vCPU host sufficient for full sweep; GPU arm requires separate host. No experiment executed; no results claimed.", "assumptions": ["Library details (JaxMARL batched MPE API, PettingZoo simple_spread_v3 defaults/parallel API, JAX behaviors) come from prior knowledge and are unverified in the offline inspected runtime; the design marks them as check-at-execution items with version pinning", "Episode-steps/s is the headline metric; agent-steps/s secondary; JaxMARL batched throughput reported as labeled num_envs × calls/s plus per-call wall-clock, per the researcher's decision 1", "Primary comparison is CPU vs CPU with JAX_PLATFORMS=cpu; GPU arm optional and conditional", "Reset cost amortized inline for JaxMARL vs per-episode reset loop for PettingZoo, with reset-only supplementary measurements to quantify the asymmetry", "PettingZoo MPE maps to one discrete action per agent per cycle; the parity harness verifies this mapping on both sides"], "uncertainties": ["Exact JaxMARL/JAX/PettingZoo versions and MPE variant naming remain unverified offline; pinning procedure handles this at execution time", "Whether the optional GPU arm is ever executed depends on host availability outside the inspected runtime", "Parity-check tolerance (rtol=1e-4 on rewards/dones, observations compared structurally) may need adjustment if internal representation ordering legitimately differs between implementations", "Batch sweep upper bound (16384) is provisional and may be adjusted to host memory at execution"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-d210723aaea242ef9a2863a45a3fd207", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-d210723aaea242ef9a2863a45a3fd207", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"answer\": \"# Experiment Design: JaxMARL MPE Simple Spread vs. PettingZoo MPE (CPU) \\u2014 Steps-per-Second Under Random Actions\\n\\n**Status: DESIGN ONLY. No experiment was executed and no results are claimed.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Research question and hypotheses\\n\\n**RQ:** How does JaxMARL's JAX-native implementation of MPE Simple Spread compare to the original CPU-based MPE (PettingZoo) in steps-per-second under uniformly random actions?\\n\\n**H1 (primary):** With JIT-compiled, vectorized stepping on CPU, JaxMARL achieves higher episode-steps/s than PettingZoo's CPU implementation at matched environment configuration.\\n\\n**H2 (secondary, batch sweep):** JaxMARL throughput increases with `num_envs` (vectorized batch size) toward a saturation point; the design identifies where diminishing returns begin on the target CPU.\\n\\n**H3 (descriptive, optional GPU arm):** On a GPU host, JaxMARL throughput exceeds the CPU numbers by a reported factor (described conditionally; not a gating claim).\\n\\n## 2. Environment configuration (parity contract)\\n\\nBoth implementations configured identically; any deviation voids the run:\\n\\n| Parameter | Value | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Agents (N) | 3 | MPE simple_spread default |\\n| Landmarks | 3 | Default (one per agent) |\\n| Episode length | `max_cycles=25` | Same for both arms |\\n| Action space | Discrete(5) | Verified by printing both sides' action spaces; PettingZoo uses MultiDiscrete([5]*3) per-agent in parallel API \\u2014 the harness maps a single 5-way action per agent per cycle on both sides |\\n| Observation space | Shape/ dtype compared at reset; both are local (pos, vel, landmark deltas, comms) continuous vectors | Shapes must match modulo known JaxMARL flattening; assert equal feature count |\\n| Random policy | Single seeded uniform sampler over {0..4}, one action per agent per cycle, identical seed streams on both sides (numpy `default_rng(seed)` on CPU; `jax.random` with equivalent seed for JaxMARL's batched action tensor) | |\\n| Seeds | 5 independent episode seeds; seed 0 used for parity check | |\\n\\n**Gating parity check (pre-benchmark, per \\u00a75 step 3):** under seed 0 and the same seeded action sequence, step both environments for one full episode (25 cycles) and compare per-cycle (a) observation shapes, (b) per-agent reward vectors, (c) termination/done flags, within float tolerance (rtol=1e-4). **If traces diverge, the benchmark is invalid.** Debug order: (1) observation construction ordering (agent/landmark indexing, which quantities are included and their concatenation order); (2) reward normalization (`local_ratio` handling \\u2014 PettingZoo blends local/global rewards; confirm JaxMARL's weighting matches); (3) termination/episode-boundary semantics (collisions, boundary conditions, `done` vs `termination`/`truncation` split in JAX envs); (4) action-space mapping (discrete one-hot vs index). Parity is evaluated on rewards and dones, not exact observations, because internal representation ordering may legitimately differ while dynamics match; if observations differ but reward/done traces match within tolerance, the benchmark proceeds with a documented note.\\n\\n## 3. Metric definitions (fixed by researcher decision)\\n\\n- **Episode-step:** one step of the environment, i.e., one parallel step across all N agents. This is the headline unit.\\n- **Agent-step:** one single-agent step (N agent-steps = 1 episode-step in MPE). Reported secondarily.\\n- **JaxMARL vectorized arm:** per call, `num_envs` episode-steps occur. Report:\\n  - **labeled batched throughput:** `num_envs \\u00d7 calls/s` episode-steps/s, always annotated with the `num_envs` used;\\n  - **per-call wall-clock:** seconds per `step()` call and per-call latency distribution, so a single-env-equivalent figure can be derived transparently.\\n- **Reset accounting (explicit asymmetry note, per researcher decision 4):**\\n  - *JaxMARL:* auto-reset is amortized inside the steady-state loop (batched envs reset inline when done, as is idiomatic for JAX vectorized envs); reset cost therefore appears inside steady-state numbers.\\n  - *PettingZoo:* reset is handled per-episode (`done \\u2192 env.reset()`); with `max_cycles=25`, a full episode includes 1 reset + 25 steps, and steady-state measurement runs many back-to-back episodes so the per-episode reset cost is also inside the steady-state number.\\n  - Because the amortization mechanics differ (batched inline vs per-episode loop), reset contributes asymmetrically; a supplementary measurement reports **reset-only cost** for each side (time `reset()` alone, \\u226550 repetitions) so the asymmetry can be quantified and, if material, a reset-excluded sensitivity figure is also reported.\\n\\n## 4. Hardware pinning\\n\\n- **Primary platform:** CPU-only. `JAX_PLATFORMS=cpu` (and `--xla_force_host_platform_device_count` left default) for the JaxMARL arm; PettingZoo runs on plain numpy threads.\\n- **Affinity:** both arms pinned to the same fixed CPU set, e.g., `taskset -c 0-15` (16 cores), with intra-process thread counts controlled: `XLA_FLAGS=--xla_cpu_multi_thread_eigen=true` and thread pool sized to the pinned set; numpy/OpenBLAS threads (`OMP_NUM_THREADS`) matched to the same count. A thread-count sensitivity spot-check (1, 4, 16 threads) is included as a secondary factor since vectorized JAX-on-CPU benefits from intra-op parallelism while numpy MPE is largely single-threaded per env.\\n- **GPU arm (optional, conditional):** executed only on a host with a working CUDA driver; same protocol with `JAX_PLATFORMS=gpu`, batch sizes scaled up (e.g., 1k\\u201365k envs), CPU-only results reported alongside. If no GPU is available (as in the inspected runtime), the design stands complete without it.\\n- Environment hygiene: fixed clock source where available, background load checked (idle machine), and `block_until_ready()` (or `jax.block_until_ready(result)`) before stopping the JAX-side clock to exclude async-dispatch distortion.\\n\\n## 5. Measurement procedure (step-by-step)\\n\\n**Per arm, per run:**\\n1. Set seed, pin affinity, instantiate environment(s) with the parity-contract config.\\n2. **Warm-up (untimed):** perform W steps (JaxMARL: enough calls to trigger JIT compilation of `reset` and `step`, plus 20 steady steps after compile; PettingZoo: 20 steps) \\u2014 discard all timing.\\n3. **Steady-state measurement:** run until either K episode-steps or a wall-clock budget T is reached (whichever first; defaults K = 2,000 episode-steps per PettingZoo run, T = 10 s per run \\u2014 both arms use the same rule). During the loop:\\n   - sample uniform random actions from the seeded policy each cycle;\\n   - JaxMARL: batched `step(actions)`; count `num_envs` episode-steps per call; auto-reset amortized;\\n   - PettingZoo: `step()` per parallel env, then per-agent reward/`done` consumption; on done \\u2192 `reset(seed=s')`;\\n   - record per-step timestamps (coarse loop-level timing is primary; per-step deltas recorded for distributional checks).\\n4. Record: total wall-clock (after `block_until_ready` on the JAX side), episode-steps completed, agent-steps, number of resets, JIT compile time (JaxMARL, from warm-up instrumentation), and `num_envs`.\\n5. **Repeats:** 5 independent runs per configuration point (fresh process per run where feasible, to isolate JIT/allocator state; at minimum fresh env instances), each with a distinct seed.\\n\\n**Batch sweep (JaxMARL only):** `num_envs \\u2208 {1, 8, 64, 256, 1024, 4096, 16384}` (log-spaced; adjusted if memory-bound on the host). PettingZoo's equivalent point is `num_envs = 1` (its native mode). This yields the H2 saturation curve and an honest apples-to-apples point at `num_envs=1` in addition to the labeled batched figures.\\n\\n**Reset-only measurement:** 50 timed `reset()` calls per arm (JaxMARL: batched at each swept `num_envs`), reported separately.\\n\\n## 6. Analysis plan\\n\\n- **Per configuration point:** mean and standard deviation of episode-steps/s across the 5 runs; also report median and min/max (throughput timings are right-skewed; the median is the robust central estimate).\\n- **Headline comparison:** JaxMARL best steady-state configuration (argmax over the batch sweep, stated explicitly with its `num_envs`) vs. PettingZoo `num_envs=1`, at `num_envs=1` vs `num_envs=1` for the implementation-only comparison. **Speedup** = median(JaxMARL steps/s) / median(PettingZoo steps/s), reported with a bootstrap 95% CI (10,000 resamples over runs).\\n- **Significance:** Welch's t-test on log-transformed per-run throughputs (\\u03b1 = 0.05), given n=5 per arm; also report the effect size as the ratio of medians with CI rather than relying on the p-value.\\n- **Batch sweep:** plot episode-steps/s and per-call latency vs `num_envs` (log-x); report saturation `num_envs` (smallest batch within 5% of peak) and throughput-per-unit-work trends.\\n- **Sensitivity figures:** reset-excluded throughput; thread-count sweep; GPU arm if available.\\n- All raw per-step timestamps and per-run summaries are emitted as CSV/JSON with config metadata (versions, seeds, thread counts, affinity) so the analysis is reproducible.\\n\\n## 7. Version pinning (conditional on execution)\\n\\nIf executed, pin and record: `jaxmarl==<X>`, `jax==<Y>`, `pettingzoo==<Z>` (with the MPE variant actually imported \\u2014 `simple_spread_v3` preferred; if only `v2` is available, run it and record the substitution as a validity note), `numpy`, Python version, and commit hashes if installed from source. A `requirements.txt` freeze accompanies results. Since library details here rest on prior knowledge (unverified offline), the design treats exact version behavior as a check-at-execution item, not an assumption.\\n\\n## 8. Controls and validity threats\\n\\n**Controls:** parity gating check (\\u00a72); identical seeds and random policy; identical config; pinned CPU set and matched thread counts; warm-up excluded; same K/T steady-state rule; reset-only supplementary measurement.\\n\\n**Threats / limitations (explicit):**\\n1. **Vectorization accounting:** batched JaxMARL steps are not equivalent work to a single PettingZoo env; mitigated by labeled reporting and the `num_envs=1` point.\\n2. **Reset amortization asymmetry** (per \\u00a73); mitigated by reset-only figures and a reset-excluded sensitivity view.\\n3. **JIT compile amortization:** compile cost is excluded from steady state but is a real one-time cost; reported separately. Single-env JIT overhead (retracing, dispatch) may dominate at `num_envs=1` \\u2014 this is itself a finding, not a bug.\\n4. **Random-action policies don't exercise all state space** (e.g., collision-heavy trajectories): acceptable for a throughput question, but noted as not a policy-learning benchmark.\\n5. **Version drift:** MPE v2 vs v3 differences; JAX version performance changes. Mitigated by pinning.\\n6. **Hardware specificity:** results are machine-specific (this design targets the inspected 224-vCPU Xeon Platinum 8570 host, CPU-only); not portable claims.\\n7. **Timing granularity:** per-step `perf_counter` overhead is negligible at the loop level but per-step distributions should be interpreted cautiously; loop-level totals are primary.\\n8. **Prior-knowledge library details** (APIs, defaults) are unverified in the offline inspected runtime and must be validated at execution (\\u00a77).\\n\\n## 9. Feasibility under the inspected runtime\\n\\nThe inspected runtime (Python 3.14.4, 224 vCPUs, ~2 TB RAM, **no GPU**, **no network**, jaxmarl/pettingzoo **not installed**) **cannot execute this benchmark as-is**: dependencies are uninstallable offline. Feasibility therefore depends on an execution environment with (a) network or pre-installed `jax`, `jaxmarl`, `pettingzoo`, `numpy`; (b) pinned CPU access. The 224-vCPU host comfortably supports the full batch sweep (16k envs of a lightweight MPE env fits easily in RAM); the GPU arm requires a different host and remains optional. Under the design-only brief this is acceptable \\u2014 the design is complete and executable wherever the dependencies exist.\\n\\n**Deliverables of a future execution:** per-run CSVs, summary statistics table (median episode-steps/s \\u00b1 std, speedup with CI, per-call latency), batch-sweep curve, reset-only timings, parity-check log, and environment freeze. No experiment has been run.\", \"assumptions\": [\"Library details (JaxMARL batched MPE API, PettingZoo simple_spread_v3 defaults/parallel API, JAX behaviors) come from prior knowledge and are unverified in the offline inspected runtime; the design marks them as check-at-execution items with version pinning\", \"Episode-steps/s is the headline metric; agent-steps/s secondary; JaxMARL batched throughput reported as labeled num_envs \\u00d7 calls/s plus per-call wall-clock, per the researcher's decision 1\", \"Primary comparison is CPU vs CPU with JAX_PLATFORMS=cpu; GPU arm optional and conditional\", \"Reset cost amortized inline for JaxMARL vs per-episode reset loop for PettingZoo, with reset-only supplementary measurements to quantify the asymmetry\", \"PettingZoo MPE maps to one discrete action per agent per cycle; the parity harness verifies this mapping on both sides\"], \"evidence\": \"Repository observations: /workspace contains only task.json confirming the design-only brief (\\\"track.md: Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\\\"); no jaxmarl or pettingzoo installed (ModuleNotFoundError for both; pip list empty of relevant packages); network disabled (pip download fails with connection error); Python 3.14.4. Hardware inspected: 224 vCPU Intel Xeon Platinum 8570, ~2011 GB RAM (1893 GB available), nvidia-smi fails (no GPU), gpu_enabled=false. Design incorporates all five researcher decisions: (1) dual metric accounting with labeled batched throughput and per-call wall-clock; (2) CPU-vs-CPU primary with conditional GPU arm; (3) shape + seeded reward/termination trace parity check as a gating control with a defined debug order (obs construction, reward normalization/local_ratio, termination semantics, action mapping); (4) explicit statement of amortized-inline reset (JaxMARL) vs per-episode reset (PettingZoo) plus reset-only supplementary timing; (5) version-pinning procedure (requirements freeze, record imported variant v2 vs v3, commit hashes if from source). Measurement protocol: 5 runs per point, warm-up excluded, block_until_ready before stopping JAX clock, steady-state rule K=2000 episode-steps or T=10s, batch sweep num_envs in {1,8,64,256,1024,4096,16384}, thread/affinity pinning with sensitivity spot-check. Analysis: median throughput with bootstrap 95% CI, Welch's t-test on log-transformed run throughputs, saturation-point identification, reset-excluded and thread-count sensitivity figures. Feasibility: inspected runtime cannot execute (no deps, no network); design executable on any host with jax/jaxmarl/pettingzoo installed; 224-vCPU host sufficient for full sweep; GPU arm requires separate host. No experiment executed; no results claimed.\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"summary\": \"Full benchmark design delivered with rationale, controls, analysis plan\", \"uncertainties\": [\"Exact JaxMARL/JAX/PettingZoo versions and MPE variant naming remain unverified offline; pinning procedure handles this at execution time\", \"Whether the optional GPU arm is ever executed depends on host availability outside the inspected runtime\", \"Parity-check tolerance (rtol=1e-4 on rewards/dones, observations compared structurally) may need adjustment if internal representation ordering legitimately differs between implementations\", \"Batch sweep upper bound (16384) is provisional and may be adjusted to host memory at execution\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-760d18e66e1048adabeaa846953560b0", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Full benchmark design delivered with rationale, controls, analysis plan", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
